title: The Assembly at Weimar
subtitle: Ebert is elected president of the Republic.
new-page: true
tags: event
view-if: year = 1919 and month = 2 and crisis = 0
max-visits: 1
on-arrival: {!
// the party grows month by month in 1919, faster when workers listen to us
// between the great clashes, the state of siege eases a little
Q.repression -= 6;
// the Left Radicals expect to win the coming congress, so they do not walk out before it
if (!Q.ultraleft_gone && Q.heidelberg_outcome == 0) { Q.ultraleft_satisfaction = Math.max(Q.ultraleft_satisfaction, 30); }
Q.militancy -= 2;
var g = Math.max(1.05, Math.min(1.35, 1.24 + Q.worker_support / 200 - Q.repression / 400));
for (var d of Q.districts) {
    Q['district_' + d + '_members'] *= g;
}
Q.government_name = 'Scheidemann cabinet (SPD, Centre, DDP)';
Q.chancellor = 'Philipp Scheidemann';
Q.spd_in_government = 1;
Q.zentrum_in_government = 1;
Q.ddp_in_government = 1;
Q.radek_prison = 1;
Q.freikorps_strength += 40;
!}

= The Assembly at Weimar

The National Assembly has met in the theatre at Weimar, far from the streets of Berlin, behind a ring of Maercker's volunteers. On 11 February it elected Friedrich Ebert president of the Republic. Philipp Scheidemann heads a coalition of the Social Democrats, the Catholic Centre and the liberal Democrats. The workers' councils that made the revolution have been thanked and sent home.

On 12 February the police arrested Karl Radek in Berlin. He is in Moabit prison, and the government talks of a Bolshevik conspiracy. [? if luxemburg_alive = 0 : The same week, Jogiches printed in *Die Rote Fahne* a photograph of the officers of the Eden Hotel celebrating after the murders. The Republic's courts show no interest. ?]

The party must say what it thinks of the new Republic.

- @expose: Expose the Assembly, and argue for the councils.
- @march: Call for a march on Weimar.
- @anchor: Demand, with the Independents, that the councils enter the constitution.
- @ignore: Ignore Weimar and build councils in the factories.

@expose
subtitle: The Spartacist line: patient criticism, for the day the workers see it themselves.
on-arrival: worker_support += 3; luxemburgist_satisfaction += 5; lean_luxemburgist += 2

Every issue of the paper takes the Assembly's debates apart. The workers who voted for Ebert read it too, and some begin to wonder.

@march
subtitle: [? if lock_uprising = 1 : Against the party line: costs Unity. ?] The Left's answer: take the revolution to Weimar. The volunteers are waiting.
choose-if: lock_uprising < 2
unavailable-subtitle: The [+ leading_faction_adj +] line forbids armed adventures.
on-arrival: {!
if (Q.lock_uprising == 1) {
    Q.unity -= 10;
    Q[Q.leading_faction + '_satisfaction'] -= 10;
}
Q.militancy += 8;
Q.repression += 5;
Q.offensive_satisfaction += 8;
Q.lean_offensive += 2;
Q.stability -= 2;
!}

A few thousand marchers set out from Erfurt and Jena. Maercker's men stop them outside the town. The Assembly debates under guard.

@anchor
subtitle: [? if lock_united_front = 1 : Against the party line: costs Unity. ?] The Right's answer. The Independents are pleased; nothing is won yet.
choose-if: lock_united_front < 2
unavailable-subtitle: The [+ leading_faction_adj +] line forbids any common front with the social democrats.
on-arrival: {!
if (Q.lock_united_front == 1) {
    Q.unity -= 10;
    Q[Q.leading_faction + '_satisfaction'] -= 10;
}
Q.uspd_relation += 8;
Q.worker_support += 2;
Q.unionist_satisfaction += 6;
Q.ultraleft_satisfaction -= 4;
Q.lean_unionist += 2;
!}

The demand for the councils to be written into the constitution spreads from Halle to the Ruhr. The Assembly promises to consider it.

@ignore
subtitle: The Left Radicals: the Republic is not our affair. The councils are.
on-arrival: ultraleft_satisfaction += 6; lean_ultraleft += 2; worker_support -= 1; militancy += 2

The Bremen and Hamburg comrades say that Weimar will collapse on its own. The councils will be ready.
